  Subject  days avg_reaction avg_residual share_above  rank
    &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
1     337    10         376.         77.2         1       1
2     308    10         342.         43.6         0.6     2
3     352    10         337.         38.9         0.9     3
4     372    10         318.         19.4         0.9     4
5     333    10         316.         17.7         0.9     5

The common line starts at 251.4 ms on day 0 and rises 10.5 ms per day.
Each residual is a subject’s reaction time minus that line’s prediction for the same day. Subject 337 was 77.2 ms slower than the line on average, and slower on all 10 days.

Subjects as random intercepts

PHP 8.5+PHP 8.3–8.4


// Days nested in subjects: a shared intercept and slope,
// and one intercept shift per subject.
$sleep = DataFrame::fromCsv("$data/sleepstudy.csv");
$formula = 'Reaction ~ Days + (1 | Subject)';
$fit = $sleep |&gt; lmer($formula);

foreach ($fit['columns'] as $i =&gt; $term) {
    printf(
        "%-12s %8.4f  (se %.4f)\n",
        $term,
        $fit['coefficients'][$i],
        $fit['std_errors'][$i],
    );
}
$subjectSd = $fit['theta'][0] * $fit['sigma'];
$icc = $subjectSd ** 2
    / ($subjectSd ** 2 + $fit['sigma'] ** 2);
printf(
    "subject sd %.3f, residual sd %.3f, ICC %.3f\n\n",
    $subjectSd,
    $fit['sigma'],
    $icc,
);

// Each subject's raw mean reaction time beside its
// estimated intercept shift.
$random = $fit['random'][0];
$shifts = array_combine(
    $random['group_labels'],
    array_column($random['effects'], 0),
);
echo $sleep |&gt;
    group_by('Subject') |&gt;
    summarize(days: n(), raw_mean: mean('Reaction')) |&gt;
    mutate(
        intercept_shift: fn ($r) =&gt;
            round($shifts[$r-&gt;Subject], 1),
        raw_mean: fn ($r) =&gt; round($r-&gt;raw_mean, 1),
    ) |&gt;
    arrange(desc('intercept_shift')) |&gt;
    head(5);


