A first script
A first pipeline on the overview page is a complete script, with the command that installs phplyr. The snippets on this page use its $penguins table. The examples in the reference pages read their data by URL from PHPstats datasets, with $data set as in that script:
$data = 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/phpstats/datasets/main/csv';
$penguins = DataFrame::fromCsv("$data/penguins.csv");

Loading data
phibble builds tables from CSV files, PDO queries, query builders, and PHP arrays:
$penguins = DataFrame::fromCsv("$data/penguins.csv");
$rows = DataFrame::fromRows([['id' =&gt; 1, 'x' =&gt; 2.5], ['id' =&gt; 2, 'x' =&gt; null]]);
$cols = DataFrame::fromColumns(['id' =&gt; [1, 2], 'x' =&gt; [2.5, null]]);
null is a missing value, R’s NA. A DataFrame is immutable, so every function returns a new table and leaves its input unchanged. echo $table prints the first rows in the format of R’s tibble package.

Columns, row closures, and vector helpers
Columns are named with strings: 'body_mass_g' where dplyr takes a bare body_mass_g.
A row closure is an ordinary PHP closure. It receives the row as a Phibble\Row, whose columns read as $r-&gt;body_mass_g or $r['body_mass_g'], and the table of the row’s group as a second argument:
mutate(bill_ratio: fn ($r) =&gt; $r-&gt;bill_length_mm / $r-&gt;bill_depth_mm)
filter(fn ($r, $group) =&gt; $r-&gt;body_mass_g &gt; mean('body_mass_g', naRm: true)($group))
A vector helper is one of phplyr’s functions for whole columns, such as lag('x'), cumsum('x'), min_rank('x'), or mean('x'). It is evaluated once per group. A summary helper in mutate() gives one value per group, and that value is repeated on every row of the group:

